2.4  Clock speed, turbo and power saving

TL;DR
  - A CPU's speed changes all the time: it climbs when there's work, drops when
    idle, and goes above its base frequency (turbo) while power and heat allow.
  - The kernel's cpufreq driver and governor pick the speed; idle CPUs drop into
    sleep states (C-states), deeper ones saving more but waking slower.
  - Too hot, and the CPU slows itself down (thermal throttling): a laptop can
    lose half its speed under a long load.

      grep MHz /proc/cpuinfo                # current speed of each CPU (x86)
      cpupower frequency-info               # driver, governor, limits
      sensors                               # temperatures (package lm_sensors)

On your machine
  Driver    intel_pstate, governor powersave
  Range     400 MHz to 4.7 GHz
  Now       400 MHz to 4.1 GHz across 8 CPUs
  Turbo     allowed
  Idle      intel_idle: POLL 51,234, C1E 2.4 million, C6 1.8 million, C8
            950,000, C10 14.2 million (CPU 0, times entered)
  CPU temp  58 °C (x86_pkg_temp)

Pitfalls
  - With intel_pstate and amd-pstate, the powersave governor still reaches full
    speed under load: it isn't "always slow".
  - Benchmarks drift as the chip heats and turbo fades: warm up first, and
    compare runs on the same power profile, plugged in.
  - A virtual machine often sees one fixed, made-up frequency: the host decides
    the real one.
  - A slow machine with a low load may be throttling: check temperatures and the
    fan before blaming software.

Deep dive

  ▾ Why not always run at full speed?

    Power grows much faster than frequency: a higher clock needs a higher
    voltage, and power rises with the square of the voltage. Running a burst
    fast and then sleeping deeply ("race to idle") often costs less energy than
    running slowly for longer. Turbo exploits the other side: the chip is
    allowed to exceed its sustained power limit for a short while, until heat
    builds up.

  ▾ What are C-states?

    C0 is running. In C1 the core stops its clock but wakes almost instantly;
    deeper states turn off caches and power, and take tens or hundreds of
    microseconds to wake. The cpuidle governor guesses how long the CPU will
    stay idle and picks the deepest state worth it. For very latency-sensitive
    work, limiting deep states trades power for reactivity.

Related
  Commands  cpupower, sensors, grep
  Paths     /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/cpufreq/, /sys/class/thermal/
  Hub       atfq cpu CPU
  See also  atfq 2.2 Cores, threads and sockets
  See also  atfq 18.1 Load average
